const faq = [
  {
    "q": "Can car ads use CGI or AI-generated images?",
    "a": "Yes. No code or ruling read for this page bans them. The UK regulator calls its code media-neutral, meaning the rules apply however the content was made, and it has upheld complaints against car ads built largely or completely in CGI. The advertiser stays responsible for whatever an AI tool produces."
  },
  {
    "q": "What must be accurate in generated automotive imagery?",
    "a": "Two layers. Rule-driven items: belted occupants, no blur or dust speed cues, no weaving, figures matched to the model shown, sourced range claims in China and labels on generated frames there. Vehicle items, which are production practice: paint, badges, grille, wheels, plates, driving side and reflections that agree with the scene."
  },
  {
    "q": "When does an automotive shoot still need a real car?",
    "a": "When reflective paint, chrome or glass carries the frame, when a named driver or contracted location is part of the brief, and for the high-touch brand moments a launch leans on. No rule cited here forces a shoot. The choice comes down to what generation still gets wrong, and reflections top that list."
  },
  {
    "q": "What are the rules for showing driving in car ads?",
    "a": "In the UK, CAP Code Section 19 and BCAP Code Section 20 bar unsafe driving and speed as the main message, and safety features can't be sold as a license to drive faster. Australia's industry code bars unsafe driving wherever it is depicted. The EU and the US regulate the fuel and emissions figures an ad prints. China's Advertising Law requires sourced data."
  },
  {
    "q": "How long does generated automotive content take?",
    "a": "One published example: the premium SUV case page reports global assets in 3 weeks, against six months traditionally, for a launch across 15 markets. That is one engagement, not a market benchmark, and no method-stated benchmark was found. Timing depends on how many markets, formats and captured assets a launch needs."
  }
];
